{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The code is explicitly throwaway and its only job is to test whether an idea is worth pursuing, so unread AI output carries no maintenance or correctness debt. Speed of exploration matters far more than quality here."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo shown to stakeholders or investors needs to actually work reliably enough to be credible, so behavior should be validated with tests or manual checks. Full acceptance without reading is risky because a visible failure in front of an audience is costly."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real early users depend on it, and correctness bugs directly hurt adoption and trust, so every change should at least be behaviorally verified. Selective review plus tests catches the costly mistakes without slowing iteration to a crawl."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and a maintaining team mean the code must be understandable, secure, and changeable over years, so a person should review every change line by line. Untraced AI code becomes permanent hidden risk in a system people rely on."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Regulated or safety-critical systems demand accountability, traceability, and defensible reasoning for every line, which only a human author can own. AI can suggest, but a person must write or rewrite the code and take full responsibility for it."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Vibe coding should be inversely proportional to the stakes and cost of being wrong: relax fully at the throwaway prototype (4), dial in behavior-focused review for demos and MVPs (3), require line-by-line human review in production (2), and keep a human as the author of record for critical systems (1). The guiding principle is that unread AI code is acceptable only where the code itself is disposable, and the required rigor grows with how long the code lives and how much it impacts real users."
}
